WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.310
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:06.570
And, uh, I'm back indoors because
it's sometimes windy outside

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:10.680
and the sun is very bright and
so it's a bit less reliable.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:11.910
So I'm back indoors.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.070
I've hooked up my microphone to my
computer where I'm recording and

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.510
using Audacity rather than relying
on the recording of the iPhone.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.820
I hope this produces better sound quality.

00:00:24.509 --> 00:00:29.550
I want to talk today and maybe in the
next couple of videos about talking and

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.470
about mistakes, because all of us, when
we learn a language, we eventually,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.620
we want to speak, uh, if we're in
a situation where we're able to use

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.730
whatever limited amount of the language
we have, you know, we'll typically do so.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.900
Certainly when I was in Japan, as
soon as I could even say anything

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.760
to anyone I would try to do so.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:52.260
In Vancouver, if I come across an Iranian
speaker, I'll try and say something in

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.505
Persian, So that's the main goal, but you
all know if you follow my videos, that

00:00:56.505 --> 00:01:01.215
I'm very much a proponent of input-based
learning, listening and reading.

00:01:01.785 --> 00:01:07.125
And the reason for that is simply that
when we're in a conversation and we don't

00:01:07.125 --> 00:01:11.715
understand what people are saying, and we
don't have any words, it's, it's painful.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:18.090
So, you know, I have started speaking
more in Persian and in Arabic, and

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:21.630
I've gone through periods where I
had a lot of online sessions, and

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.570
online sessions with tutors are great.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.620
Actually, it's a phenomenal
capability that we have today.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:33.660
And I want to salute italki which
has a wide range of teachers in

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.120
a tremendous range of languages.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.240
So I can connect with
people, for example, in Iran.

00:01:39.825 --> 00:01:41.295
Uh, from different cities.

00:01:41.295 --> 00:01:44.565
So I'm getting to know different cities
in Iran, and they talk about where they

00:01:44.565 --> 00:01:48.975
live and the food they eat and, and all
the while I'm speaking and in order to

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:51.345
speak well, you do have to speak a lot.

00:01:51.345 --> 00:01:52.905
There's no getting around it.

00:01:52.905 --> 00:01:57.135
And, uh, as you continue with
your input-based activities,

00:01:57.135 --> 00:01:58.755
at least that's my strategy.

00:01:59.145 --> 00:02:02.985
As I listen more and I read more and
I acquire more words and I understand

00:02:02.985 --> 00:02:05.610
more then more and more I want to speak.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:10.200
And as I go to speak, I, I discover
that, um, a lot of the words that

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.650
I've learned, I can't remember them
when I go to speak, but the more I

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.930
speak, the better I get at speaking.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.800
So we do have to speak a lot.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.720
And I even think that in order to
speak a language really well and

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.680
not be in danger of losing it you
have to possibly spend time in the

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.970
country where the language is spoken.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:38.190
You need to speak so much that, that
you can eventually get as a sufficient

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:42.989
hold on that language that it won't,
it won't be frittered away by, by lack

00:02:42.989 --> 00:02:48.319
of use, because I know that when I go
back into my Greek, Mini Stories are,

00:02:48.679 --> 00:02:52.200
uh, you know, Mini Stories in Romanian
that there's a lot of words that I'm

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.010
going to have to learn again, because
I've forgotten them, which isn't a

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.410
problem to me because I have them there.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.549
I can go back to them.

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:02.609
When I learn them again, I will
eventually know them better.

00:03:03.315 --> 00:03:08.055
However speaking is a phenomenal
way of learning the language.

00:03:08.325 --> 00:03:11.385
Now, some people suggest you
should speak from day one.

00:03:11.595 --> 00:03:15.225
I've never quite understood that
because day one you know nothing.

00:03:15.345 --> 00:03:20.695
So even if you engage in a
conversation, all you're doing is,

00:03:20.695 --> 00:03:23.445
is, is, well, you can't say anything.

00:03:23.475 --> 00:03:27.734
Uh, the person you're speaking to has to
explain everything that they're saying.

00:03:28.304 --> 00:03:31.755
And my experience has been that when
I'm in a conversation and especially if

00:03:31.755 --> 00:03:36.240
I don't understand very much, and then
the tutor tells me this is the word

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.070
for such-and-such, I can't remember it.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.510
It's impossible to remember.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:45.300
Uh, I find that it's much more useful
for me to hear it than to read it

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.920
and hear it again and read it again
and gradually allow the brain to

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.600
get used to the language that way.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:55.680
But when you start speaking, whenever
that may be early in your learning or

00:03:55.710 --> 00:04:00.645
later in your learning, listening is a
big part of speaking, paying attention

00:04:00.825 --> 00:04:04.875
to what the speaker, and especially
if it's a native speaker, is saying.

00:04:04.935 --> 00:04:10.515
So it's still very much an input-based
activity and the less of the language

00:04:10.515 --> 00:04:16.065
you already have in you, the more of
an input-based activity the speaking

00:04:16.065 --> 00:04:22.725
is, but it still can be quite painful,
uh, if you are with people and you

00:04:22.725 --> 00:04:24.555
simply don't understand anything.

00:04:25.125 --> 00:04:27.885
I guess there are people who
believe in this speak from

00:04:27.885 --> 00:04:29.385
day one and who practice it.

00:04:29.595 --> 00:04:31.065
I don't know how that works.

00:04:31.425 --> 00:04:37.245
Uh, I have been, you know, uh, had, uh,
tutoring engagements at an early stage of

00:04:37.245 --> 00:04:39.555
learning a language and I find it painful.

00:04:39.555 --> 00:04:42.825
I can't wait until the session
is over because I can't really

00:04:42.825 --> 00:04:43.935
understand what they're saying.

00:04:43.935 --> 00:04:45.795
And I can't find any words.

00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:50.805
At least if I have learned the words
through my input activities and can't

00:04:50.805 --> 00:04:56.234
remember them, at least I have a hope
of eventually remembering them, but if

00:04:56.234 --> 00:05:00.705
I have no idea what these words are, and
then they are given to me in the class,

00:05:01.005 --> 00:05:02.565
there is no way I can remember them.

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:08.804
So of course, one of the things that
I do in my sessions with tutors is I

00:05:08.804 --> 00:05:15.554
asked them the tutors to, uh, produce,
uh, a text file with a list of 10 or

00:05:15.554 --> 00:05:18.015
15 or however many words they can.

00:05:18.645 --> 00:05:20.685
Ideally they do it during the lesson.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:24.330
So it doesn't become an extra
10, 15 minutes work for them.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:25.890
All the words...

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:30.840
can't be all, but the words that I
used incorrectly, or couldn't remember,

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.760
or wasn't able to use or phrases, if
they can create a list of these 10,

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.970
15, 20, and in an ideal scenario,
record them, then send them to me.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:46.650
Then I import these as lessons into
LingQ and, and, you know, I can then

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:48.150
review these over and over again.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:48.900
I can review.

00:05:49.425 --> 00:05:56.775
Uh, you know, the, these reports from a
lesson two months prior and gradually get

00:05:56.775 --> 00:05:59.535
to know these words and start to use them.

00:05:59.985 --> 00:06:02.835
But so the talking it's important to talk.

00:06:03.315 --> 00:06:06.165
I think it's exciting now that I've
spoken to a couple of tutors in

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:12.195
Iran recently, one lives in Esfahan
one lives by the Caspian sea.

00:06:13.409 --> 00:06:16.500
See there again, she gave me, we
must've mentioned the name of the city

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.270
where she lives three or four times.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:20.540
I haven't seen it written out yet.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.159
... maybe I can't remember it.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:27.240
You know, we have to recognize that
it's not just because I'm old, you

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.620
can't remember stuff like that.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:32.400
Uh, and so, but at any rate, I
have a tutor from Esfahan, a tutor

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.440
from up there near the Caspian sea.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:35.799
I tutor from Tehran.

00:06:36.075 --> 00:06:40.435
The tutor from up North, she
is originally from Larestan,

00:06:40.455 --> 00:06:42.094
which is somewhere else in Iran.

00:06:42.285 --> 00:06:46.395
So you're talking to people, you're
getting an understanding of the country,

00:06:46.395 --> 00:06:49.605
of what is going on in that country,
of the geography of the country.

00:06:49.605 --> 00:06:55.005
You're using words, you know, ideally
you're using words that you've learned.

00:06:55.125 --> 00:06:56.775
You're picking up a few words.

00:06:57.315 --> 00:07:00.675
You're practicing the art of
speaking because to speak well,

00:07:00.675 --> 00:07:02.175
you have to practice speaking.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.300
Uh, you, can't just all of a sudden
stop, you know, you've been listening

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.960
and reading now and engaging, you
know, with all the good things

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.450
that, that I do at LingQ and you
just go there and start speaking.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:13.410
It doesn't work that way.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:18.000
However, my experience has been that
you can activate it pretty quickly.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.800
And very often I will study a language
with the purpose of going to the country.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:24.030
And I did that in Romania.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:24.990
I did that for Greek.

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:28.320
I did it with Czech and
with Russian, Ukrainian.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.940
And when you're there
and you're able to...

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:30.780
You're sort of.

00:07:31.095 --> 00:07:35.385
Immersed in it and, and, and working
hard to try to keep up with the

00:07:35.385 --> 00:07:40.055
conversation that there is a sort of
a white heat there that, you know,

00:07:40.245 --> 00:07:42.555
pushes you forward to that next level.

00:07:42.945 --> 00:07:46.905
But, but you have to have a certain
minimum in order to take advantage

00:07:46.905 --> 00:07:48.750
of that, say going into the country.

00:07:48.929 --> 00:07:53.159
And similarly, before you can engage
with a tutor in a meaningful way, in my

00:07:53.159 --> 00:07:58.530
opinion, you have to have invested in,
uh, you know, listening and reading.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:03.539
And then these, uh, sessions where
you're talking, they are becoming an

00:08:03.539 --> 00:08:07.590
opportunity then for you to go back
to your listening and reading and pay

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:09.510
attention to things that you missed.

00:08:09.929 --> 00:08:12.000
So it's important to talk.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.400
I don't think it matters when you talk.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.290
Some people like to talk early.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.560
I don't think there's such a thing as
talking from day one, because someone

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:22.080
has to give you somebody say, here's
how we say "hello, how are you?"

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:23.310
Then you say, "hello, how are you?"

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:24.660
It has to start with listening.

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:25.830
It doesn't start with speaking.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:31.230
And so the only question is how much
listening and reading do you want

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:32.610
to do before you start speaking?

00:08:33.015 --> 00:08:36.284
And that will depend on people's
inclinations, that will depend on

00:08:36.284 --> 00:08:40.245
their situation, on their needs,
but at whatever point you started

00:08:40.245 --> 00:08:44.775
speaking, a big part of the speaking
is still listening and paying attention

00:08:44.985 --> 00:08:46.575
and getting the language into you.

00:08:46.755 --> 00:08:51.285
Even as you practice the art of
speaking and get better at retrieving

00:08:51.285 --> 00:08:52.635
the words and phrases that you have.

00:08:52.995 --> 00:08:57.435
So that's all I wanted to say about
speaking, and I'm going to leave a

00:08:57.435 --> 00:09:00.375
couple of videos you might look at
that are sort of relevant to this.

